    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d263">

                <pb facs="0355" n="247" sameAs="frus:frus1893/pg247"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Tsui Kwo Yin to
                        Mr. Gresham</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Chinese Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Washington, D.
                                C.</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1893-03-25"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">March 25, 1893</hi></date>. (Received March
                        27.)</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the receipt
                    of your note of the 21st instant, in which you advise me that the request
                    presented in my note of the 13th instant has been considered by the
                    Attorney-General of the United States, and that he will be glad to concur in the
                    motion to advance a test case in the Supreme Court involving the
                    constitutionality of the Chinese prohibition and exclusion law, known as the
                    “Geary act,” and that he will also do all in his power to secure the advancement
                    of the case and its early determination.</p>
                <p>The willingness of the Attorney-General to cooperate with counsel representing
                    the Chinese in such a case will be communicated to Mr. Heber J. May, Washington,
                    D. C., counsel to this legation, and through him to the counsel individually
                    representing my people in the United States.</p>
                <p>Accept, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Tsui Kwo Yin</hi>.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
